718 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/T (Comparison of baseline PID and refined adaptive-PID performance. The refined controller shows significantly reduced line-error amplitude and smoother tracking behaviour, validating early stability improvements. To address spur identification, we explored multiple prototype solutions. One involved turning into every spur candidate and using the ToF to confirm authenticity. This was mechanically disruptive and inefficient. Another considered adding a second ToF or a left-facing ToF to detect corridor width directly; although technically sophisticated, it added hardware overhead and introduced bandwidth concerns on the I2C bus. The final adopted solution was a hybrid multi-sensor classification mechanism using QTR junction-width detection, asymmetric left-right IR readings, and a left-side ultrasonic measurement to distinguish open corridors from solid walls. This provided a reliable real-spur classifier without additional complex hardware. Navigation performance was then improved through a two-mode architecture. SAFE mode provided a conservative baseline using fixed speeds and classical PID for guaranteed repeatability. AGGRO mode implemented a layered control strategy. First, dynamic speed scaling used instantaneous QTR error to determine whether the robot was in straight-line, gentle-curve, or sharp-turn conditions and adjusted speed accordingly. )
/E (Comparison of baseline PID and refined adaptive-PID performance. The refined controller shows significantly reduced line-error amplitude and smoother tracking behaviour, validating early stability improvements. To address spur identification, we explored multiple prototype solutions. One involved turning into every spur candidate and using the ToF to confirm authenticity. This was mechanically disruptive and inefficient. Another considered adding a second ToF or a left-facing ToF to detect corridor width directly; although technically sophisticated, it added hardware overhead and introduced bandwidth concerns on the I2C bus. The final adopted solution was a hybrid multi-sensor classification mechanism using QTR junction-width detection, asymmetric left-right IR readings, and a left-side ultrasonic measurement to distinguish open corridors from solid walls. This provided a reliable real-spur classifier without additional complex hardware. Navigation performance was then improved through a two-mode architecture. SAFE mode provided a conservative baseline using fixed speeds and classical PID for guaranteed repeatability. AGGRO mode implemented a layered control strategy. First, dynamic speed scaling used instantaneous QTR error to determine whether the robot was in straight-line, gentle-curve, or sharp-turn conditions and adjusted speed accordingly. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [15 16 17]
>>
endobj
719 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/T (Oscillation envelope of line-error magnitude before and after dynamic-speed scaling and adaptive PID tier selection. The refined controller shows faster damping and reduced peak-to-peak variation. Second, adaptive PID employed three different tiers of Kp and Kd gains selected based on error magnitude. )
/E (Oscillation envelope of line-error magnitude before and after dynamic-speed scaling and adaptive PID tier selection. The refined controller shows faster damping and reduced peak-to-peak variation. Second, adaptive PID employed three different tiers of Kp and Kd gains selected based on error magnitude. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [18 19]
>>
endobj
