722 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Our autonomous subsystem for the NRL Skill Challenge was engineered through a structured, multi-stage process that transitioned from a minimal baseline controller to a highly optimized AGGRO architecture. The earliest prototype consisted of a basic PID line follower using the QTR-8RC array, a front VL53L0X ToF for distance measurements, two frontal IR sensors for edge discrimination, and a simple state machine managing line following, spur entry, pickup, drop, lap updates, and termination. Initial on-field evaluations revealed key deficiencies: inconsistent line stability in curves due to a single global base speed, unreliable spur identification, high overshoot during pickup and drop, and slow recovery after transient line loss.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [21 22]
>>
endobj
723 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/T (PID motor command output over time. Adaptive PID and tier-based gains produce smoother, less turbulent motor signals compared to the baseline controller, improving overall stability and responsiveness. Third, a soft-landing ToF profile used distance bands to control approach velocity, allowing precise pickup and drop without collision. )
/E (PID motor command output over time. Adaptive PID and tier-based gains produce smoother, less turbulent motor signals compared to the baseline controller, improving overall stability and responsiveness. Third, a soft-landing ToF profile used distance bands to control approach velocity, allowing precise pickup and drop without collision. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [23 24]
>>
endobj
